    slug: "you-went-into-content-mode",
    title: "You went into content mode",
    description:
      "The reason your idea list runs dry and your posts feel forced is usually not a shortage of ideas but a wall you built between living and making content, where content is a separate activity you clock into later at a desk and produce from a blank screen, so the genuinely interesting thing that happened in your real day (you explained something clearly, a first attempt went wrong in an interesting way) walks out of the room unfilmed because filming was not what you were doing right then, and hours later you manufacture something flatter to fill the slot; the reframe is that a manufactured video is a guess about what a stranger will find interesting (and people judge their own guesses badly) while a caught moment already passed a real-world test, it was interesting enough to actually happen and to want to say out loud, so it is free evidence you threw away by not recording (Gary Vaynerchuk&apos;s document-don&apos;t-create names the move, capture what you are already doing rather than fabricate something to perform, and the deeper reason it works is that caught material comes pre-checked, not merely that it is easier); content mode also makes the made version worse because the mode-switch performs, and viewers lean toward what feels real over what feels produced (Stackla: 60 percent call unpolished, regular-people content the most authentic, 16 percent say the same of produced brand content), so the rough thirty seconds you caught can beat the concept you engineered; the fix is not more ideas but shrinking the gap between doing the thing and filming it, capture first and decide later, because the interesting moment is perishable and by the time you are in content mode it has cooled into a flat note you cannot revive. Distinct from the-idea-drought-is-a-format-problem (a repeatable container for a blank post), your-next-video-is-in-your-comments (mining the audience for ideas), stop-making-more-distribute-what-you-have (distribute one idea rather than make more), decided-before-you-filmed-it (idea quality sets the ceiling the edit cannot raise), and the-editing-tax (the edit is the throughput bottleneck): this is about the mental wall between living and making that sends you to manufacture content from nothing while the caught, pre-validated material of your real day goes unfilmed.",
