    title: "You were one purchase away from starting",
    description:
      "Shopping for the camera, mic, or lights you keep meaning to buy feels like investing in your videos but is one of the most reliable ways to make none of them, because a purchase can only ever hand you capability and the thing that actually makes videos better is reps and response, which no box contains (you buy those one uncomfortable post at a time, and there is no model that skips the line: Danny Boyle&apos;s 75 million dollar 28 Years Later, the biggest film made to date on smartphones, was shot on adapted iPhone 15 Pro Max cameras, so the gap between your videos and the ones you admire was never the sensor); the buying feels like progress because it gives you the clean concrete sensation of having done a thing (money left, a box is coming) that practice never gives on day one, which is the engine behind gear acquisition syndrome, where for beginners especially buying new gear feels like a shortcut to professional results that skips the vulnerable part, the learning, and perfectionism gives procrastination a respectable outfit by turning avoidance into preparation; the way to tell a tool from an alibi is which came first, the buying or the reps (a tool removes a friction from work already happening, the clip mic that ends a re-shoot you actually do weekly, while an alibi is a purchase you set before the reps as the condition for starting, once I have the camera I will post properly), and the alibi is sticky because it is a precondition you can actually meet, so you stay busy satisfying a requirement instead of doing the thing it was for, and a new requirement is always waiting; the fix is to set the floor once and low (a recent phone, enough light, sound close to your mouth) and make the next one on what you have, because the real cost of the upgrade is the months of videos you did not make while getting ready to make them well. Distinct from the-editing-tax (the edit is the throughput bottleneck that caps output), no-one-is-watching-you-that-closely (spotlight effect drives over-polishing work that already exists), consistency-is-a-systems-problem (design a low-friction loop so a tired you shows up), and you-are-waiting-to-be-qualified (the expertise precondition and perishable fresh confusion): this is about a purchasable precondition, gear, as productive-feeling avoidance, and buying capability standing in for building it.",
