    slug: "you-kept-the-part-that-cost-you-the-most",
    title: "You kept the part that cost you the most",
    description:
      "The part of a video you are proudest of is usually the part that took the longest (the intro reshot eight times, the b-roll you drove across town for), and it is often the exact part a stranger scrolls past, because the work a piece of a video took you is disconnected from how good it is for the person watching, so the effort you sank into it is the one input steering your keep-and-cut decisions that should count for nothing. This is the sunk cost fallacy (Arkes and Blumer 1985: theater patrons who paid full price for season tickets attended more plays over the first half of the season than those given a random discount, same shows and seats, the ones who had sunk more unable to let it go to waste), and an edit is an unusually pure trap for it because the cost is loud and felt (your Tuesday night, remembered in detail) while the value is silent (a flicker of interest in a stranger who has not watched yet and might never), so when one side of the scale shouts and the other is mute you weigh the shouting one and the shot that cost the most feels worth the most; the tell is a reason for keeping something that starts with the time you have in it (I already spent three hours on this). It pushes two opposite ways and both hurt: some nights it will not let you stop, so you keep pouring hours into a video that was dead on the first pass rather than bin it and start better (the Concorde, funded for years after it was clear it would never pay for itself because quitting felt like admitting the spending was wasted), and other nights it makes you ship a merely-fine video because throwing out a whole evening is unbearable in a way posting a weak video is not (and you call it discipline, but the sunk evening decided, not you); both feel like conviction and neither is. The fix is a trick that deletes the lying number: before keeping a shot or posting the thing, imagine someone else shot the footage and handed it to you free this morning, then decide cold whether you would keep it or put your name on it, because the viewer already lives in that world (they did get it free, they never felt your eight takes, they judge the thing in front of them as if it fell out of the sky), so the only person still paying for the hours is you, in worse calls about what to keep and what to send. Distinct from posting-consistently-is-the-only-thing-that-matters (the feed cannot see how long a video took, so polish does not buy reach), you-watched-it-until-you-hated-it (repetition corrupts your judgment of the video), you-are-the-worst-judge-of-your-own-videos (confidence does not predict performance), and decided-before-you-filmed-it (the idea sets the ceiling the edit cannot raise): this is about effort-based attachment distorting the keep-cut and ship-or-kill decisions, and the fix of making the forward call blind to what it already cost.",
