    description:
      "The instinct to apologize for a gap in posting (sorry I have been gone, I am back now) assumes a room of people who noticed you were away and were waiting for you to return, and for almost everyone that room does not exist: on the format built for reach most views come from strangers (around 55 percent of Reels views come from non-followers), and even the followers you have mostly do not see any given post (a typical post reaches only a few percent of them), so the next viewer is usually someone the app handed your clip to and not a regular who came looking; the comeback apology is therefore aimed at an audience that lives only in your head, and it spends the opening seconds that decide whether a real stranger stays on your own guilt, so the fix is to delete it and just start the video; deeper, the guilt itself is misbuilt, because letting people down is a broken promise and you made no promise to a crowd that was never waiting on one, and reframing an ordinary quiet stretch as a betrayal is how a surprising number of accounts quietly end (not a decision to stop but a gap recast into something you have to face), while the real cost of a gap is small and mechanical (the app cools on you, you are a few reps rusty, both fixed by posting again); consistency still matters but for a reason that survives a bad month (the app forgets accounts that go dark and reps make you better) rather than the fragile one (a fanbase depending on you to keep your word), because guilt-based consistency collapses at the first miss while treating each post as a cheap new try in front of new people does not. Distinct from no-one-is-watching-you-that-closely (the spotlight effect on flaws inside a video), you-picked-a-pace-you-could-not-keep (the cadence number is too high and the all-or-nothing reaction to a miss), you-keep-making-episode-one (feeds show non-followers, so build self-contained episodes to earn a return), and posting-consistently-is-the-only-thing-that-matters (frequency beats polish, show up often): this is about the imagined waiting audience itself, and how believing a room of people is expecting your next post drives the comeback apology and turns a normal gap into a reason to quit.",
