    description:
      "Most people who burn out on posting did not lose motivation, they moved it somewhere it could not survive, by switching the reason they make things from an inside one (the making was fun) to an outside one (the count), which is the exact swap the overjustification effect punishes: a reward for something you already enjoy does not stack on top of the wanting but can replace it (Lepper, Greene and Nisbett 1973: preschoolers who already loved drawing and were promised a prize for it later drew about half as much on their own once the prize stopped, while the surprise-reward and no-reward groups were unchanged; a 128-experiment review by Deci, Koestner and Ryan 1999 found the same shape in adults), so once views become the reason rather than a side effect, the original fuel goes quiet; the trade is bad because the numbers are an unreliable paymaster that mostly pays nothing early on (most posts do an ordinary number, one lands maybe once a month on no schedule) while the making paid every single time win or lose, so you swap a fuel that is always in the tank for one that is mostly empty right when you need it full, and the tell is being unable to enjoy a video you are proud of because it only did 800 views, the activity now hostage to an outcome you do not control; the fix is not to ignore the numbers but to demote them to feedback read after the fact (what landed, so the next one is sharper) and keep them out of the job of being your reason to make at all, protected by making some things you would have made even if you knew no one would see them (the pre-post test: would I be glad I made this even if it did nothing). Distinct from posting-consistently-is-the-only-thing-that-matters (protect the parts of the process you enjoy, cut the edit tax) and consistency-is-a-systems-problem (design a low-friction loop so a tired you shows up) and you-picked-a-pace-you-could-not-keep (the cadence number is too high) and you-called-a-full-room-nothing (a real count feels small against shown viral outliers): this is about the source of motivation itself, and how making the numbers your reason extinguishes the enjoyment that carried you.",
