    description:
      "Somewhere between having a real opinion and posting it, you talk yourself down to the safe version (soften the claim into a maybe, post the plainer of two ideas), and it feels like being careful rather than afraid, like a professional not saying something reckless with their name on it, but the thing you are guarding is a reputation you do not have yet, and almost every cautious move a small creator makes only makes sense for one who is not small; play out what a bold post that misses actually costs and it is close to nothing, because the feed tests every post on a small batch first and pushes it wider only if they stay, whatever your follower count (a few-thousand-follower TikTok account averages around 860 views a post), so a swing that lands flat is half-watched by a few dozen people who forget it by the next video and is buried by tomorrow, the same quiet disappearance the safe version was headed for anyway; the reason the tame version still wins is loss aversion (Kahneman and Tversky: the pain of a loss runs about twice as strong as the pleasure of an equal gain), a good instinct when you have something to lose but one that never checks whether there is anything there to protect, so on a small account you flinch from a loss that cannot happen and run famous-person defense on an unknown-person account; flipped over, the obscurity that feels like the problem is exactly what makes a swing cheap, a budget for being wrong in public that shrinks as you grow and that you keep spending on the post nobody could object to, which is also the post nobody has a reason to care about (the feed carries the thing that is not the middle), so caution charges you twice, for the swing you skipped and the flat thing you posted instead; the fix is to catch the small moment you reach for the tamer version, notice you are guarding a standing that is not there, and post the one you are a little embarrassed by, because at your size it costs almost nothing and it is the only kind of post that builds the reputation you were protecting. Distinct from no-one-is-watching-you-that-closely (the spotlight effect, that others barely notice your flaws, so stop over-polishing), you-told-them-it-was-bad (the self-handicapping disclaimer that pre-excuses a flop), you-are-not-afraid-of-strangers (the first audience being close people is the real fear), and nobody-follows-a-tip (safe checkable tips fail to convert): this is about mis-pricing the risk of a bold post by imagining a reputation you have not built, and obscurity as a spendable asset that only gets more expensive to use.",
