    slug: "you-reread-the-bad-comment",
    title: "You reread the bad comment",
    description:
      "A video does fine, forty kind comments and one unkind one, and the only comment you reread that night is the mean one, which is not a flaw or a confidence problem but negativity bias (Baumeister&apos;s bad-is-stronger-than-good review: bad feedback lands harder and lasts longer than equal good, and Ito and Cacioppo&apos;s 1998 study found the brain fires a stronger response to a negative image than an equally intense positive one within a fraction of a second, before conscious judgment), so the tally in your head is rigged and forty-to-one can feel like a loss. The sting is convincing because criticism really is more specific than praise, but specific is not the same as right or common, and the comment box already skews negative because irritation reliably makes people type while quiet approval does not, so the one who spoke is usually the person least like the audience you are making things for. The real cost is not the bad evening but the next video, which you make a little safer without ever deciding to (cutting the line the critic would mock, adding the caveat, filing off the edge that made it yours), until a year of small reasonable flinches leaves an account shaped around the one person who was never going to like you and away from the many who showed up. The fix is not a thicker skin but changing what you let count (a single harsh comment is a feeling, a repeated pattern is a fact), delaying any reaction a day so the sting fades and only real information survives, and reading your saves and kind comments with the same care you would spend on the mean one. Distinct from the-silence-is-not-an-empty-room (misreading silence and over-weighting the vocal minority when reading a room) and you-keep-making-what-worked-last-time (the internal reward loop drifting output toward past hits): this is about the emotional asymmetry of a single negative comment and how negativity bias quietly steers your future work toward your critics.",
