    description:
      "You started strong, five posts the first week, four the next, then a missed couple of days in week six that felt like blowing it, and a month later the account went quiet, so you filed it under lost motivation, when the run really ended on day one in the calm moment you decided how often to post and picked a number too big for your actual life. The number was not yours, it was absorbed (post daily, three to five times a week, and TikTok itself pushing one to four times a day), and those figures describe people who make content for a living or brands with a person whose whole job is the account, so their daily sits inside a workday built around posting while yours sits on top of a job and a commute and everyone who needs you, and the big number was not even buying much because the real jump in results is from one post a week to somewhere between two and five and it flattens fast after, so the grind that wore you down was mostly climbing past the point where more posting paid you back. The pace felt reasonable because you did the math on your best day, picturing the easy twenty-minute version and not the redone take, the caption that would not come, the worn-out night, which is the planning fallacy (Kahneman and Tversky: we lowball our own tasks even remembering the last overrun, and the twist is it only hits our own estimates, outsiders guess high), so the schedule was built on a fantasy week and broken the day you wrote it, and one bad Tuesday found it out. Then you miss a day, as any real schedule guarantees, and the ambitious number turns one skip into a verdict (you broke the streak, you are not someone who follows through), which is where the harm is and it is aimed wrong, because a single missed day barely dents the long run (Lally&apos;s habit study) and what ends things is quitting in response to the miss, the all-or-nothing story a too-high pace manufactures on a loop until you believe it. The fix is to set the number for the tired Wednesday-in-month-four version of you rather than the rested Sunday planner, pick the pace that person could keep (almost always lower), accept it will feel like too little while everyone shouts daily, because a slow pace you hold beats a fast one you abandon since the abandoned one goes to zero, and build in slack, bank a couple ahead, decide in advance a miss is allowed. Distinct from consistency-is-a-systems-problem (design a low-friction loop so a tired you shows up) and posting-consistently-is-the-only-thing-that-matters (frequency beats polish): this is about the cadence number itself being the error, set by the planning fallacy and a full-time reference class, and the fix being to deliberately under-commit to your worst-week pace.",
