      "You wanted to make a certain kind of video, looked, saw the niche was already full of people doing it well, and quietly decided there was no room for you, so you either never started or went hunting for an emptier lane to skip the competition, which felt like the smart strategic move and is usually the expensive one. The fear runs on a picture of a niche as a fixed pie, where every new creator makes everyone&apos;s slice thinner, but that picture is wrong, because a viewer does not follow one creator per topic, they follow a stack of them and watch whichever the feed serves next, so your arrival does not carve up a set audience, it drops one more good video in front of people whose appetite for the thing was never one creator deep. Most Reels views (around 55 percent) come from non-followers and reach tilts toward smaller accounts over the big ones, so a packed niche is not a queue where you wait behind the incumbents, and with more than 200 million people making content against an even larger appetite, room was never the scarce thing. An empty niche is rarely a gap the world missed, it is usually a room with no audience in it, because crowds form where the watching is, so fleeing competition and fleeing viewers are mostly the same move, while a crowded niche has already built the road (a trained audience, a platform that knows who to show it to, visible winning shapes), so you are merging onto moving traffic instead of cutting a trail. The only thing the crowd truly threatens is your sameness, fixed by being one specific person in a full niche rather than the sole occupant of an empty one. Distinct from you-cannot-pick-your-niche-before-you-post (a niche is discovered by posting a spread, not chosen up front), you-checked-if-it-was-taken (a single idea already done killing one post), late-to-every-trend (a trend is time-bound and front-loaded), and someone-already-has-your-audience (collaborating to borrow rooms other people filled): this post is about rejecting a whole niche for being crowded, and the fixed-pie fear that makes an empty lane look safer than a proven one.",
