    slug: "you-copied-what-you-could-see",
    title: "You copied what you could see",
    description:
      "You watch a video take off, study exactly what it is doing (the cut on the beat, the captions snapping in, the pause before the payoff), rebuild those moves, and your version does nothing, so you decide the format is burned out, when you never had the format, only its costume. Every video has two layers, the delivery on the surface (cuts, captions, sound, the words of the hook) and the reason underneath that made any of it worth watching (a real stake, or one specific person saying the thing only they would say), and the surface is loud and easy to copy while the reason is quiet and hard to point at, so learning from a hit by copying what you notice aims you at the surface every time. This is not laziness but how people read any example (Chi, Feltovich and Glaser, 1981: physics beginners sorted problems by how they looked, inclined planes in one pile and pulleys in another, while experts sorted by the invisible principle that solved them, and with video nearly everyone is the beginner), and it is Feynman&apos;s cargo cult, the Pacific islanders who cleared runways and lit the fires and sat a man in a wooden hut with carved headphones and still saw no planes land, because the runway was never what brought them. The cost is the false verdict: your copy flops, you cross the move off as played out, but you cannot wear out a thing you never actually did, and the version with something real inside it is the one you just quit. The fix is to copy one level down, asking not what did they do but what were they giving me and why did I stay, then putting your own version of that underneath, since the invisible layer you kept scrolling past was the only part that was ever going to be yours. Distinct from studying-what-works-is-making-you-worse (exposure to winners converges everyone on the same middle), late-to-every-trend (a trend is already saturated by the time you can see it), and you-checked-if-it-was-taken (originality fear killing the post before it exists): this is about copying the visible layer of a video that worked and missing the invisible reason it did.",
