      "You figured something out last month, thought about explaining it to whoever is still stuck there, then decided you were not qualified yet and did not make it, when that instinct is almost exactly backwards. The person who knows a subject cold is often the worst one to explain it, because of the curse of knowledge (Elizabeth Newton&apos;s tappers heard a whole song in their head while listeners heard only knocking, and guessed listeners would name it about half the time when they managed 2.5 percent) and its classroom form the expert blind spot, where a thing going automatic deletes your access to what it was like not to know it, so you skip the exact step the beginner needed. What makes you a good explainer here is not how much you know but that you still remember not knowing, and that memory is the most perishable thing you own: the confusion fades the moment the thing becomes easy and does not come back, so the clearest video you will ever be able to make about this is the one available right now, while the struggle is fresh, and it decays every week you wait. You are not waiting to get better (you already did the thing), you are waiting for a permission that never arrives, and the viewer is not auditing your years, they are asking did this help me, which the near voice one step ahead answers better than the distant authority. Distinct from you-are-making-it-for-the-wrong-person (an expert mis-aiming above beginners to impress peers), you-told-them-it-was-bad (the self-handicapping disclaimer), and you-have-good-taste (the taste-skill gap): this is about the expertise precondition people set before they are allowed to start, and the perishable value of their own fresh confusion.",
