      "You keep starting over, a new account or the old one wiped clean, a new name and a first post that swears this time it is for real, and every time it feels like the responsible move when it is actually the most expensive thing you can do, because starting over resets the two things that only compound with time: a back catalog that keeps getting resurfaced by search and returning subjects, and the slow recognition that turns a scattered stranger into an audience by making your face and name stop reading as new. The pull to restart is a documented thing, the fresh-start effect, where a temporal landmark files your failures under a previous version of you (which is exactly why a new account feels like relief even though nothing about the work changed), and the cruel part is that the urge hits hardest right when the current thing is closest to working, because that is when the flat stretch feels most unfair and the growth building underneath has not surfaced yet, so the restart is really quitting a few weeks before the turn, dressed up as commitment, and doing it every few months keeps you on video one forever. The fix is to see that a pivot is a turn and not a demolition: you can change direction, change the subject, get more serious, all while keeping the account, the record, and the people who already know you, because the patience that looks like talent from the outside is mostly just the refusal to reset the count to zero.",
